{
  "about": "The vibrational bands expected of a mineral's chemistry: per group and mode, the free-ion fundamental and the range the band takes in minerals (cm-1). Hand-maintained. 'free' is the fundamental of the free ion; 'range' is where the band of that mode is usually found in a crystal, where site symmetry splits the degenerate modes and bonding shifts them. Where published mineral descriptions give enough positions (band_windows.json, built from a corpus of papers) the tool shows that measured window beside the range. An entry tagged 'check' was entered from memory of the cited source and has not been compared with it line by line: treat it as a guide and correct it here. A mode tagged 'broad' is a wide region every hydrous mineral has bands in (librations, M–O–H bends): it is offered after the modes that stand at a position of their own. 'shape' is the free ion's geometry, which fixes what each numbered mode IS and where it is active — tetrahedral XO4 (Td): ν1 symmetric stretch (A1, Raman), ν2 symmetric bend (E, Raman), ν3 antisymmetric stretch (F2, Raman + infrared), ν4 antisymmetric bend (F2, Raman + infrared); planar XO3 (D3h): ν1 symmetric stretch (A1′, Raman), ν2 out-of-plane bend (A2″, infrared), ν3 antisymmetric stretch (E′, both), ν4 in-plane bend (E′, both); pyramidal XO3 (C3v): ν1 symmetric stretch, ν2 symmetric bend (both A1), ν3 antisymmetric stretch, ν4 antisymmetric bend (both E), all active in both; linear OXO (D∞h): ν1 symmetric stretch (Raman), ν2 bend (infrared), ν3 antisymmetric stretch (infrared). 'active' is the free ion's selection rule: in a crystal the site symmetry is lower and a mode forbidden in the free ion commonly appears, weakly.",
  "sources": {
    "N": "Nakamoto K. (2009) Infrared and Raman Spectra of Inorganic and Coordination Compounds, Part A, 6th ed. Wiley — free-ion fundamentals",
    "F": "Farmer V.C., ed. (1974) The Infrared Spectra of Minerals. Mineralogical Society Monograph 4 — ranges in minerals",
    "C": "Čejka J. (1999) Infrared spectroscopy and thermal analysis of the uranyl minerals. Reviews in Mineralogy 38, 521–622 — uranyl",
    "L": "Libowitzky E. (1999) Correlation of O–H stretching frequencies and O–H···O hydrogen bond lengths in minerals. Monatshefte für Chemie 130, 1047–1059 — O–H stretching",
    "R": "Ross S.D. (1974) in Farmer (1974): borates, sulfates and other oxyanions"
  },
  "activity": {"R": "Raman", "IR": "infrared", "R+IR": "Raman and infrared"},
